{"name":"Lazy Day","hex":"#95aed1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lazy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unwilling to do work or make an effort; disinclined to exertion.","Causing or characterised by idleness; relaxed or leisurely."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To laze, act in a lazy manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lazy"},{"term":"day","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2 & ISO 639-5 language code for Land Dayak languages."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time when the Sun is above the horizon and it lights the sky.","A period of time equal or almost equal to a full day-night cycle, being 24 hours long."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/day"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lazy Days (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Lazy Days is a 1929 Our Gang short comedy film, the 89th in the series, directed by Robert F. McGowan."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Lazy_days_TITLE.JPEG/330px-Lazy_days_TITLE.JPEG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazy_Days_(film)"},{"title":"The Sooty Show","blocks":[{"text":"The Sooty Show is a British children's television series, created by Harry Corbett, and produced for the BBC from 1955 to 1967, and then for ITV from 1968 until 1992. The show, part of the Sooty franchise, focuses on the mischievous adventures of the glove puppet character of the same name, alongside his friends Sweep and Soo, and their handler. Between 1955 and 1975, Corbett presented the programme until his retirement, before it was taken over by his son Matthew Corbett. It also co-starred Marjorie Corbett as the voice of Soo from the character's debut in 1964, until her retirement in 1981, whereupon Brenda Longman replaced her."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2a/Sootyshow1983al.jpg/330px-Sootyshow1983al.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sooty_Show"},{"title":"Lazy Day (Spanky and Our Gang song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Lazy Day\" is a song written by Tony Powers (lyrics) and George Fischoff (music), and recorded by the 1960s band Spanky and Our Gang. It appeared on their album Spanky and Our Gang."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1a/Lazy_Day_-_Spanky_%26_Our_Gang.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazy_Day_(Spanky_and_Our_Gang_song)"},{"title":"Day","blocks":[{"text":"A day is the time period of a full rotation of the Earth with respect to the Sun. On average, this is 24 hours. As a day passes at a given location it experiences morning, afternoon, evening, and night. This daily cycle drives circadian rhythms in many organisms, which are vital to many life processes."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Day"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Midtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg/500px-Midtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Midtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg/1280px-Midtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Midtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg/500px-Midtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMidtown_Manhattan_from_Weehawken_September_2021_panorama_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Midtown_Manhattan_from_We